{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics has a lot of weird stuff \n- but there’s one thing that everyone agrees"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that no one understands. I’m talking about\nquantum spin. Let’s find out how chasing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this elusive little behavior of the electron\nled us to some of the deepest insights into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15360,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nature of the quantum world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a classic demonstration done in\nundergraduate physics courses - the physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "professor sits on a swivel stool and holds\na spinning bicycle wheel. They flip the wheel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over and suddenly begin to rotate on the \nchair. It’s a demonstration of the conservation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of angular momentum. The angular momentum \nof the wheel is changed in one direction,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the angular momentum of the professor has \nto increase in the other direction to leave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44800,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the total angular momentum the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Believe it or not, this is basically the same\nexperiment - suspend a cylinder of iron from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a thread and switch on a vertical magnetic\nfield. The cylinder immediately starts rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a constant speed. At first glance this\nappears to violate conservation of angular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum because there was nothing spinning \nto start with. Except there was - or at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there sort of was. The external magnetic field \nmagnetized the iron, causing the electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the iron’s outer shells to align their\nspins. Those electrons are acting like tiny"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bicycle wheels, and their shifted angular\nmomenta is compensated by the rotation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86160,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cylinder."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That explanation makes sense if we imagine \nelectrons like spinning bicycle wheels - or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spinning anything. Which might sound fine\nbecause electrons do have this property that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we call spin. But there’s a huge problem:\nelectrons are definitely NOT spinning like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bicycle wheels. And yet they do seem to possess \na very strange type of angular momentum that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somehow exists without classical rotation.\nIn fact the spin of an electron is far more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental than simple rotation - it’s\na quantum property of particles, like mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the various charges. But it doesn’t just\ncause magnets to move in funny ways - it turns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out that quantum spin is a manifestation of a \nmuch deeper property of particles - a property"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is responsible for the structure of all\nmatter. We’ll unravel all of that over a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136960,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "couple of episodes - but today we’re going to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138320,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to talk about what \nspin really is and get a little closer to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142080,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding what this weird property of nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The experiment with the iron cylinder is called \nthe Einstein de-Haas effect, first performed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by, well, Einstein and de-Haas in 1915. It\nwasn’t the first indication of the spin-like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties of electrons. That came from looking \nat the specific wavelengths of photons emitted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when electrons jump between energy levels \nin atoms. Peiter Zeeman, working under the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great Hendrik Lorenz in the Netherlands, found \nthat these energy levels tend to split when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atoms are put in an external magnetic field. \nThis Zeeman effect was explained by Lorentz"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "himself with the ideas of classical physics.\nIf you think of an electron as a ball of charge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving in circles around the atom, that motion \nleads to a magnetic moment - a dipole magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field like a tiny bar magnet. The different\nalignments of that orbital magnetic field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195520,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative to the external field turns\none energy level into three."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds reasonable. But then came the anomalous \nZeeman effect. In some cases, the magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field causes energy levels to split even further \n- for reasons that were, at the time, a complete"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mystery. One explanation that sort of works is \nto say that each electron has its own magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moment - by itself it acts like a tiny bar\nmagnet. So you have the alignment of both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the orbital magnetic moment and the electron’s \ninternal moment contributing new energy levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for that to make sense, we really need\nto think of electrons as balls of spinning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge - but that has huge problems.\nFor example, in order to produce the observed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic moment they’d need to be spinning \nfaster than the speed of light. This was first"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pointed out by the Austrian physicist Wolfgang \nPauli. He showed that, if you assume electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a maximum possible size given by the best \nmeasurements of the day, then their surfaces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would have to be moving faster than light\nto give the required angular momentum. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s assuming that electrons even have\na size - as far as we know they are point-like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- they have zero size, which would make the \nidea of classical angular momentum even more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267280,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nonsensical. Pauli rejected \nthe idea of associating such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271120,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a classical property like rotation to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron, instead insisting on calling\nit a “classically non-describable two-valuedness”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280480,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so electrons aren’t spinning, but somehow \nthey act like they have angular momentum. And this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is how we think about quantum spin now. It’s \nan intrinsic angular momentum that plays into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the conservation of angular momentum like \nin the Einstein de-Haas effect, and it also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives electrons a magnetic field. An electron’s \nspin is an entirely quantum mechanical property,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and has all the weirdness you’d expect from \nthe weirdest of theories. But before we dive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into that weirdness, let me give you one more \nexperiment that reveals the magnetic properties"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313760,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that result from spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Stern-Gerlach experiment - proposed \nby Otto Stern in 1921 and performed by Walther"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gerlach a year later. In it silver atoms are\nfired through a magnetic field with a gradient"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- in this example stronger towards the north \npole above and getting weaker going down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A lone electron in the outer shell of the\nsilver atoms grants the atom a magnetic moment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the external magnetic field induces a \nforce on the atoms that depends on the direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that these little magnetic moments are pointing \nrelative to that field. Those that are perfectly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aligned with the field will be deflected by\nthe most - either up or down. If these were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "classical dipole fields - like actual tiny\nbar magnets - then the ones that were only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "partially aligned with the external field\nshould be deflected by less. So a stream of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silver atoms with randomly aligned magnetic \nmoments is sent through the magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might expect a blur of points where the \nsilver atoms hit the detector screen - some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deflected up or down by the maximum, but most \ndeflected somewhere in between due to all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the random orientations. But that's not what’s \nobserved. Instead, the atoms hit the screen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in only two spots corresponding \nto the most extreme deflections."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s keep going. What if we remove the\nscreen and bring the beam of atoms back together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we know that the electrons have to be\naligned up or down only. Let’s send them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through a second set of Stern-Gerlach magnets, \nbut now they’re oriented horizontally. Classical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dipoles that are at 90 degrees to the field\nwould experience no force whatsoever. But"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we put our detector screen we see that\nthe atoms again land in two spots - now also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420400,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oriented horizontally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So not only do electrons have this magnetic \nmoment without rotation, but the direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427440,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the underlying magnetic \nmomentum is fundamentally quantum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431120,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The direction of this \"spin\" property"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is quantized - it can only take on specific\nvalues. And that direction depends on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction in which you choose to measure it. \nHere we see an example of Pauli's two-valuedness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manifesting as something like the direction \nof a rotation axis, or the north-south pole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449360,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the magnetic dipole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually this two-valuedness is far deeper \nthan that. To understand why we need to see"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how spin is described in quantum mechanics. It \nwas again Pauli who had the first big success"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here. By the mid 1920s physicists were very \nexcited about a brand new tool they’d been"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "given - the Schrodinger equation. This equation \ndescribes how quantum objects behave as evolving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distributions of probability - as wavefunctions.It \nwas proving amazingly successful at describing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some aspects of the subatomic world. But the \nequation as Schrodinger first conceived it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "did not include spin. Pauli managed to fix\nthis by forcing the wavefunction to have two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "components - motivated by this \nambiguous two-valuedness of electrons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500000,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The wavefunction became a very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strange mathematical object called a spinor, \nwhich had been invented just a decade prior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And just one year after Pauli’s discovery, \nPaul Dirac found his own even more complete fix"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Schrodinger equation - in this case\nto make it work with Einstein’s special"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of relativity - something we’ve discussed \nbefore. Dirac wasn’t even trying to incorporate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin, but the only way the equation could\nbe derived was by using spinors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530720,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now spinors are exceptionally weird and cool, \nand really deserve their own episode. But"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let me say a couple of things to give you\na taste. They describe particles that have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very strange rotation properties. For familiar \nobjects, a rotation of 360 degrees gets it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to its starting point. That’s also\ntrue of vectors - which are just arrows pointing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552560,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in some space. But for a spinor you need to \nrotate it twice - or 720 degrees - to get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559440,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to its starting state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s an example of spinor-like behavior.\nIf I rotate this mug without letting go my"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566080,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arm gets a twist. A second rotation untwists\nme."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can also visualize this with a cube attached \nto nearby walls with ribbons. If we rotate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cube by 360 degrees, the cube itself is\nback to the starting point, but the ribbons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a twist compared to how they started. \nAmazingly, if we rotate another 360 - not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "backwards but in the same direction - we get \nthe whole system back to the original state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another thing to notice is that the cube can \nrotate any number of times, with any number"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596640,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of ribbons attached, and it never gets tangled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So think of electrons as being connected to \nall other points in the universe by invisible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strands. One rotation causes a twist, two\nbrings it back to normal. To get a little"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more technical - the spinor wavefunction has \na phase that changes with orientation angle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and a 360 rotation pulls it out of \nphase compared to its starting point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622720,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get some insight into what spin really is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think not about angular momentum, \nbut regular or linear momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630080,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A particle's momentum is fundamentally \nconnected to its position."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By Noter's theorem, the invariance \nof the laws of motion to changes in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordinate location gives us the law of the \nconservation of momentum. For related reasons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643280,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in quantum mechanics position and \nmomentum are conjugate variables."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meaning you can represent a particle wavefunction \nin terms of either of these properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651520,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by Heisenberg's uncertainty principle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing your knowledge of one, means \nincreasing the unknowability of the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If position is the companion variable of momentum, \nwhat's the companion of angular momentum?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it's angular position. In other \nwords the orientation of the particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669360,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So one way to think about the \nangular momentum of an electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672560,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is not from classical rotation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674960,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather from the fact that they \nhave a rotational degree of freedom"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677920,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which leads to a conserved \nquantity associated with that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They have undefined orientation, but \nperfectly defined angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some physicists think that spin is \nmore physical than this. Han Ohanian,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691920,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "author of one of the most used quantum textbooks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shows that you can derive the right values of the \nelectron spin angular momentum and magnetic moment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by looking at the energy and charge \ncurrents in the so called Dirac field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's the quantum field surrounding \nthe Dirac spinor aka the electron,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709200,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "imply that even if the electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is point like, it's angular momentum can arise \nfrom an extended though still tiny region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However you explain it, we have an excellent \nworking definition of how spin works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that particles described by spinors\nhave spin quantum numbers that are half-integers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- ½, 3/2, 5/2, etc. The electron itself has\nspin ½ - so does the proton and neutron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their intrinsic angular momenta can only\nbe observed as plus or minus a half times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739280,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the reduced Planck constant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740960,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "projected onto whichever direction \nyou try to measure it. We call these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles fermions. Particles that have integer \nspin - 0, 1, 2, etc. are called bosons, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "include the force-carrying particles like\nthe photon, gluons, etc. These are not described"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by spinors but instead by vectors, and behave \nmore intuitively - a 360 degree rotation brings"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762160,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them back to their original state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764080,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This difference in the rotational \nproperties of fermions and bosons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767840,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results in profound differences"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in their behavior - it defines how they interact \nwith each other. Bosons, for example, are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to pile up in the same quantum states,\nwhile fermions can never occupy the same state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780960,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This anti-social behavior of fermions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manifested as the Pauli Exclusion \nPrinciple and is responsible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for us having a periodic table, for electrons \nliving in their own energy levels and for matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792800,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually having structure. It’s the reason"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you don’t fall through the floor right now.\nBut why should this obscure rotational property"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead to such fundamental behavior? Well this \nis all part of what we call the spin statistics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theorem - which we’ll come back to in an\nepisode very soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons aren’t spinning - they’re doing\nsomething far more interesting. The thing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we call spin is a clue to the structure of\nmatter - and maybe to the structure of reality"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820480,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself through these things we call spinors\n- strange little knots in the subatomic fabric of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828160,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time we talked about the connection between \nquantum entanglement and entropy - this was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a heady topic to say the least, but you guys\nhad such incredibly insightful comments and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838640,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Joseph Paul Duffey asks whether entropy is \nan illusion created by our observation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "isolated components within a \"larger\" \nentangled system? Well the answer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that entropy is sort of relative. \nIt's high or low depending on context. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854640,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The air in a room may be perfectly mixed and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so considered “high” entropy. But if that room \nis warm compared to a cold environment outside,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the total room + environment is at a\nrelatively low entropy compared to the maximum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868960,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- if you opened the doors and \nlet the temperature equalize."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Von Neumann entropy is different to thermodynamic \nentropy in that it represents the information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contained in the system and extractable in\nprinciple, versus information that’s lost"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882960,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the system by entanglement with the \nenvironment with the environment. On  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885680,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other hand, classical or \nthermodynamic entropy represents"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information that is hidden beneath the crude \nproperties of the system, but may in principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be extracted. And yet von Neumann entropy has \na similar contextual nature. If your system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has no entanglement with the environment then \nits von Neumann entropy is zero. But if you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consider a subsystem within that \nsystem then that entropy rises."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Randomaited asks the following: If entropy\nonly increased over time, which implies it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was at its minimum at the Big Bang, does that \nmean there was no quantum entanglement at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Big Bang? To answer this we’d need to\nknow why entropy is so low at the Big Bang - and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s one of the central mysteries of the\nuniverse. But, I’ll give it a shot anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we can’t really talk about the t=0 beginning \nof time, because that moment lost in our ignorance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936640,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about quantum gravity and inflation and whatever \nother crazy theory we haven’t figure out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet. But what we do know is that at some very, \nvery small amount of time after t=0, the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was extremely compact - which meant hot and \ndense, and it was also extremely smooth. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compact part is where the low entropy comes \nfrom. The “gravitational degrees of freedom”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were almost entirely unoccupied. On the other \nhand, the extreme smoothness meant that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entropy associated with matter was extremely \nhigh. Energy was as spread out as it could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get between all of the particles and the different \nways they could move. The low gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973520,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entropy massively outweighed the matter entropy, \nso entropy was low. That smoothness seems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to suggest the particles of the early universe \nwere already entangled - otherwise how did"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985760,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they spread out their energy?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chris Hansen makes the same point, asking if \nthe conditions of the Big Bang meant everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993520,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started out entangled. You’d think so - but \nthat’s not necessarily the case. Remember"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000080,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that von Neumann entropy is relative to the "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002400,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system you’re talking about, \nand so is entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005440,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say you have a bunch of particles\nthat are not entangled with each other but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are all entangled with another bunch of particles \nsomewhere else. If you ignore those other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles then it seems like there’s no\nentanglement in the particles of the first"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017680,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet those particles may have correlated \nthermodynamic properties due to their mutual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connection to the outside. In the early\nuniverse, the extreme expansion of cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation may have permanently separated entangled \nregions, but left those regions with an internal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thermal equilibrium which does NOT require maximal \nentanglement within the regions themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, the universe - or our patch\nof it - may have started out unentangled and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046160,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at low entropy, even if it was at thermal\nequilibrium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lincoln Mwangi also dropped some knowledge, \ninforming us that “The Cloud” - is actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "named after Dr, Shannon, the founder of the \nfield of information theory. As with many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these things, the word has been corrupted \nover time and is now routinely mispronounced."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is very disrespectful, and I intend to\nwrite a series of op-eds to correct the matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070240,
    "dDurationMs": 9600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right after we upload this video to the Claude."
    } ]
  } ]
}
